{
  "definition": "Generalization error is the difference between the training error and the test error, or the training error and the population error."
}

{
  "definition": "Pointwise non-linearity is a type of nonlinear function used in neural networks to map outputs. These non-linearities are typically applied to each component of the output vector individually."
}

{
  "statement": "In adversarial domain adaptation, is the model trained to perform well on the primary task while simultaneously confusing the domain classifier?",
  "explanation_true": "Yes, the model is trained to maximize task performance while minimizing its ability to distinguish the data's origin, forcing the representations to align.",
  "explanation_false": "No, the model is only trained to maximize performance on the target domain data, ignoring the source domain and the adversarial component of the training process."
}

{
  "definition": "Embedding vector is a univariate function of the input data $x$, meaning it is a function of $x$ alone. This contrasts with a distance metric, which is a bivariate function of two data points, $x_1$ and $x_2$."
}

{
  "prompt": "Explain the concept of visual prompting in foundation models. How does this method of adaptation differ from traditional fine-tuning or modifying the model's internal parameters?",
  "reference_answer": "Visual prompting involves modifying the image encoder by adding a visual prompt, such as a learned border or beneficial noise, to the input image. This process seeks to find what can be added to the image to maximize the likelihood that the model correctly maps the category. Unlike traditional fine-tuning, which modifies the model's weights, visual prompting focuses on adapting the input. This approach is valuable because it can be used even when the underlying model weights or logits are not available.",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "modifying the image encoder",
      "keywords": [
        "modifying the image encoder",
        "visual prompt"
      ]
    },
    {
